  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ELEMENT 6 — COMMON PITFALLS AND HOW TO AVOID THEM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="alg-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common IRMAA Appeal Letter Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Submitting Form SSA-44 Without a Cover Letter — Your Appeal Gets Buried</h3>
    <p>Social Security offices receive hundreds of forms daily. A bare Form SSA-44 with no cover letter provides no immediate context for the reviewer. A professional cover letter identifying your Medicare ID, explaining your life event in clear terms, and listing the enclosed documentation gets your appeal reviewed faster — and reduces the likelihood of a "returned for additional information" notice.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to always attach a signed cover letter explaining your situation in plain language, referencing your IRMAA determination notice number, and listing every document you are enclosing. Use this tool to generate the letter in 2 minutes.</p>

    <h3>Filing the Wrong Form — SSA-44 vs. SSA-561</h3>
    <p>Form SSA-44 and Form SSA-561 serve completely different purposes. Form SSA-44 is for requesting a new income calculation when your income genuinely dropped due to a qualifying life event. Form SSA-561 (Request for Reconsideration) is for challenging SSA's use of incorrect data — for example, if they used the wrong tax year or applied the wrong MAGI figure. Filing SSA-561 when you need SSA-44 will result in denial and cost you weeks of processing time.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to use Form SSA-44 for any income reduction due to retirement, spousal death, divorce, reduced work, or pension loss. Download the correct form at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/forms/ssa-44.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSA.gov Form SSA-44 (PDF)</a>.</p>

    <h3>Sending Documentation via Regular Mail — Losing Your 60-Day Filing Proof</h3>
    <p>Regular first-class mail provides no proof of when you sent your appeal or whether SSA received it. If your packet is lost in transit or delayed at a high-volume SSA office, you have no evidence that you filed within the 60-day window from your IRMAA determination notice. Missing the 60-day window can complicate retroactive refund eligibility.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to always use USPS Certified Mail with Return Receipt Requested (green card). The certified mail tracking number and the signed green return receipt card together prove: (1) the date you mailed it, and (2) the date SSA received it. Keep both permanently.</p>

    <h3>Stopping Medicare Premium Payments During the Appeal — Creating a Medicare Enrollment Problem</h3>
    <p>Some seniors, frustrated with paying IRMAA surcharges during the appeal review period, stop paying or underpay their Medicare Part B premium. This is a serious mistake. Non-payment of Medicare premiums can trigger a premium delinquency notice and, in extreme cases, threaten your Medicare enrollment status — a consequence far more costly than the IRMAA surcharge you're appealing.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to continue paying your full Medicare premium (including the IRMAA surcharge) every month during the appeal process. If SSA approves your appeal retroactively, any overpayments will be refunded as a credit against future premiums. See official premium payment requirements at <a href="https://www.medicare.gov/basics/costs/medicare-costs/part-b-costs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Medicare.gov Part B Costs</a>.</p>
  </section>

